WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.226
Data often crosses several boundaries
before a Go application trusts it.

2
00:00:04.306 --> 00:00:08.092
A value may move from a request into a
handler, from a handler into a service,

3
00:00:08.172 --> 00:00:12.199
from a service into a database, and later
from the database back into a response.

4
00:00:12.279 --> 00:00:16.425
Each transition is a chance to preserve or
lose important assumptions about type,

5
00:00:16.505 --> 00:00:18.489
ownership, encoding, and allowed use.

6
00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:21.874
For database work, prefer safe APIs that
separate code from data.

7
00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:26.281
Parameterized operations and query
builders can help keep data values distinct from

8
00:00:26.361 --> 00:00:27.262
query structure.

9
00:00:27.342 --> 00:00:31.429
The application should validate data
before storage and before downstream use,

10
00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:35.716
because stored data may later appear in
reports, messages, exports, or responses.

11
00:00:35.796 --> 00:00:39.221
A field that was harmless in one context
may be sensitive in another.

12
00:00:39.301 --> 00:00:43.087
Serialization and deserialization are also
trust-boundary operations.

13
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:46.172
JSON, XML, CSV, protocol buffers, message
queue payloads,

14
00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:50.098
and third-party responses should be
decoded according to clear contracts.

15
00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:53.303
Unknown fields, oversized payloads,
unexpected encodings,

16
00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:56.448
and partially valid records should be
handled predictably.

17
00:00:56.528 --> 00:01:01.036
Avoid letting convenience decoding
silently create broad, trusted application state.

18
00:01:01.116 --> 00:01:03.039
File handling deserves the same care.

19
00:01:03.119 --> 00:01:06.124
Validate expected file size, type,
location, and purpose.

20
00:01:06.204 --> 00:01:08.968
Avoid trusting user-provided names as
storage paths.

21
00:01:09.048 --> 00:01:11.212
Keep temporary file cleanup predictable.

22
00:01:11.292 --> 00:01:14.236
Treat uploads, configuration files,
exported archives,

23
00:01:14.316 --> 00:01:16.600
and generated files as data with a
lifecycle.

24
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.947
A defensive Go application keeps data
contracts clear as information moves between

25
00:01:21.027 --> 00:01:23.070
storage, memory, files, and responses.

26
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.876
Data security often depends on context, so
preserve that context in code.

27
00:01:26.956 --> 00:01:30.803
A value that is safe for an internal
identifier may not be safe for a file name,

28
00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:32.926
log field, export, or external API call.

29
00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:37.153
Naming internal types clearly, separating
storage models from response models,

30
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:41.259
and documenting important transformations
help teams avoid accidental trust

31
00:01:41.339 --> 00:01:41.940
expansion.
